{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"a94ca9ce-91d6-43df-a568-85bdc0542f07","step":{"id":"s1","script":"A pendulum swings because gravity pulls its displaced bob back toward the lowest position. The angle measures displacement from vertical.","ops":[{"op":"diagram","id":"pendulum","region":"left","primitives":[{"kind":"point","at":[0.5,0.15],"label":"pivot"},{"kind":"line","points":[[0.5,0.15],[0.5,0.73]],"stroke":"dashed"},{"kind":"line","points":[[0.5,0.15],[0.72,0.68]],"stroke":"solid","label":"L"},{"kind":"ellipse","center":[0.72,0.68],"radius":[0.035,0.035],"stroke":"solid","fill":true},{"kind":"arc","center":[0.5,0.15],"radius":[0.17,0.17],"start_deg":67.5,"end_deg":90,"stroke":"solid","label":"θ"}]},{"op":"text","id":"heading","region":"C1","content":"Pendulum: small-angle motion"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.warning","request_id":"a94ca9ce-91d6-43df-a568-85bdc0542f07","code":"step_repaired","step_hint":"s2"}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"a94ca9ce-91d6-43df-a568-85bdc0542f07","step":{"id":"s2","script":"Only gravity's tangential component restores the bob. For small angles in radians, sine theta is approximately theta, making the restoring force proportional to displacement.","ops":[{"op":"arrow","id":"restoring","canvas_id":"pendulum","from":[0.72,0.68],"to":[0.58,0.738],"stroke":"solid","label":"restoring"},{"op":"equation","id":"force","anchor":{"el":"heading","side":"below","gap":0.06},"latex":"F_t=-mg\\sin\\theta\\approx-mg\\theta"},{"op":"equation","id":"smallangle","anchor":{"el":"force","side":"below","gap":0.05},"latex":"\\text{For }|\\theta|\\text{ much smaller than }1\\text{ radian}"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"a94ca9ce-91d6-43df-a568-85bdc0542f07","step":{"id":"s3","script":"Since arc displacement equals length times angle, Newton's law gives angular acceleration opposite to displacement. That is the defining pattern of simple harmonic motion.","ops":[{"op":"point","id":"equilibrium","canvas_id":"pendulum","at":[0.5,0.723],"label":"equilibrium"},{"op":"equation","id":"motion","anchor":{"el":"smallangle","side":"below","gap":0.06},"latex":"s=L\\theta\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad\\ddot\\theta=-\\frac{g}{L}\\theta"}],"checkpoint":{"question":"When the bob is displaced to the right, which way is its tangential acceleration?","expected_gist":"Toward equilibrium, to the left; acceleration opposes displacement."}}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"a94ca9ce-91d6-43df-a568-85bdc0542f07","step":{"id":"s4","script":"The angular frequency is square root of g over L. Longer pendulums swing more slowly; mass does not affect the small-angle period.","ops":[{"op":"equation","id":"period","anchor":{"el":"motion","side":"below","gap":0.06},"latex":"\\omega=\\sqrt{\\frac{g}{L}},\\qquad T=2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{L}{g}}"},{"op":"angle_arc","id":"swingpath","canvas_id":"pendulum","center":[0.5,0.15],"radius":0.49,"start_deg":67.5,"end_deg":112.5,"stroke":"dashed"},{"op":"text","id":"limit","anchor":{"el":"period","side":"below","gap":0.05},"content":"Small swings: period nearly independent of amplitude"}],"checkpoint":null}}
